<title>USA: U.S. Nov producer prices rose 0.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Nov producer prices rose 0.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-12-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Nov    Oct   Nov96/95</p>
<p> Finished Goods		0.4    0.4     3.0</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1   -0.3     0.6</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -0.1    0.8     3.7</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 2.3    1.9    12.9</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.5  132.5     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Nov    Oct   Nov96/95</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     2.8    5.5    33.4</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		 -0.4    3.8    29.4</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	  -0.1    0.3     2.3</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     0.5   -1.6    -0.6</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	  0.3   -0.4     0.3</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  0.2   Unch     0.6</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1   -0.1    -1.2</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	Unch   -0.2    -1.7</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.4   -0.3     1.8</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Nov    Oct   Nov96/95</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    1.2    1.7    11.2</p>
<p> Crude Goods		   1.8   -0.6     9.4</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X     -0.3    0.3    -6.5</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -1.9   -2.7     3.2</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	  4.7    1.1    14.4</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	 7.7    1.5    30.5</p>
<p> -Petroleum		   -4.6    8.5    39.6</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast November producer prices to rise 0.3 percent. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.2 percent rise.</p>
<p>The department said the rise in finished energy goods prices in November was the largest since a 2.7 percent jump in April 1996. It also said that residential gas prices surged 3.8 percent in November, the biggest monthly increase on record.</p>
